  "findings": [
    "Real intent (101 cases, one day): Qwen MLX 8-bit 79 argmax correct vs Laya 47. Under the 0.8 policy: 70 right / 18 wrong / 13 deferred vs Laya 45 / 20 / 36.",
    "Qwen's 18 real errors: 12 false wakes (10 room or TV fragments with nothing playing, 2 while the agent was talking) and 6 misses (3 'can you hear me?' checks, 3 questions mixed into echo of its own reply). Laya's 20: 15 misses, 5 false wakes. A confident ignore is not rescued by the mic-check rule, which only answers unsure decisions.",
    "On the 9 real cases the user labelled by voice: Qwen 6 right / 3 wrong / 0 deferred; Laya 4 / 2 / 3.",
    "Synthetic intent: acceptance 40/40, heldout 48/48 argmax (Laya 37 and 43); echo regressions 8/8 automatic (Laya 3 + 5 deferred).",
    "Answers: 59/60 synthetic heldout vs the regex 44/60; real 11/12 for both (the regex was tuned on these 12 replies, so they are not blind for it).",
    "Style: no clear winner. Real argmax 84/119 vs Laya 78; under the policy 71 right / 22 over-rejected / 26 deferred vs 65 / 21 / 33. Both over-reject plain spoken updates.",
    "Speed: real-intent prompts (median 356 tokens) p50 96 ms / p95 118 ms on MLX 8-bit vs Laya's two passes p50 102 / p95 130. Short synthetic prompts 35-45 ms vs Laya 29-32 ms.",
    "Footprint: 1.75 GB peak process footprint vs Laya 3.87 GB; model load 0.6 s vs 31 s (one measurement each). Download 645 MB vs Laya's 846 MB bundle.",
    "4-bit costs accuracy (MLX 4-bit real intent 65 / 20 / 16, real answers 9/12); 8-bit matches the fp16 reference (70 / 18 / 13).",
    "Household speech in training (local-only run) moved real intent to 82 argmax and 13 automatic errors (vs 78 and 18 for the same quant): a per-user local fine-tune is the next lever, not a shipped model.",
    "MLX's buffer cache must be capped: uncapped, a 4-bit run's process footprint reached 15.1 GB over the 462 cases."
  ],
  "recommendation": {
    "verdict": "ship",
    "model": "qwen-syn-pb2 (no household speech in training)",
    "format": "MLX 8-bit, group size 64",
    "replaces": "the Laya voice-intent head, the acks.answer() regex for 'was that for me?' replies and the spoken-style head, on Apple Silicon. Name routing (acks.address) and the echo prefilter stay.",
    "runtime": {
      "apple_silicon": [
        "mlx==0.32.4",
        "mlx-metal==0.32.4",
        "mlx-lm==0.32.2",
        "transformers==5.20.1",
        "tokenizers==0.23.3",
        "numpy==2.5.4"
      ],
      "note": "mlx==0.32.4 is already pinned for Parakeet STT, so the wheel is shared. Set mx.set_cache_limit(256 MB) in the worker."
    },
    "other_platforms": "keep Laya until the same weights are measured on Linux CPU through the existing torch runtime (bf16 safetensors, LAYA_PINS); measured here only on MPS: p50 104 / p95 124 ms, 2.7 GB footprint.",
    "not_llama_cpp": "GGUF Q8_0 had the best latency (p50 87 ms) and footprint (0.59 GB) but llama-cpp-python 0.3.36 has no wheel for this Mac on PyPI; uv compiled it from source, which every user would repeat.",
    "threshold": "keep minimum_probability 0.8"
  },
  "distribution_plan": {
    "huggingface": "one model repo holding mlx-8bit/ (config.json, model.safetensors, model.safetensors.index.json, tokenizer.json, tokenizer_config.json, generation_config.json), the LoRA adapter and a model card with the exact prompts and label tokens. The manifest pins it like the Laya bundle: revision (commit sha), base_url resolve/<revision>/mlx-8bit/, and size + sha256 per file.",
    "github_mirror": "a GitHub Release (tag voice-qwen-v1) on the kollab repo carrying the same files as assets. The manifest adds the release download URL as a second base URL; the downloader tries Hugging Face first, then the mirror, and accepts bytes only when size and sha256 match, so the mirror can never serve different weights.",
    "update": "a new model is a new HF commit + a new release tag + new pins in manifest.json; old pins keep resolving."
  },
  "caveats": [
    "One test day and 101 real intent cases; 92 of those labels are the author's annotations, 9 are the user's own answers.",
    "Recent-chat context for real cases is reconstructed from earlier groups labelled 'respond' and the agent's spoken replies (teacher forcing), close to but not identical to the runtime's 6-message window.",
    "Latency is from a shared machine with live services; numbers are per case in one process, warm, after model load.",
    "No runtime wiring was done: the worker, manifest pins and the downloader mirror are untouched."
  ],
  "reproduce": [
    "python bench/qwen_voice.py train --playback --out ADAPTER",
    "python -m mlx_lm fuse --model Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B --adapter-path ADAPTER --save-path FUSED",
    "python -m mlx_lm convert --hf-path FUSED --mlx-path FUSED-8bit -q --q-bits 8 --q-group-size 64",
    "python bench/qwen_voice.py score mlx --model FUSED-8bit --out SCORES.json",
    "python bench/qwen_voice.py score laya --model ~/.kollab/voice/models/laya/1c5edc17a7acd8701df6fc341c0d179f1c62c982 --out LAYA.json",
    "python bench/qwen_voice.py report SCORES.json LAYA.json"
  ]
}
